  "question": {
    "text": "<math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi></math> has a smaller first ionization enthalpy than <math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></math>. Consider the following statement:<br />(I) it is easier to remove <math><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi></math> electron than<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></math>&nbsp; electron<br />(II) <math><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi></math> electron of <math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi></math> is more shielded from the nucleus by the inner core of electrons than the <math><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></math> electrons of <math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></math><br />(III) <math><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></math> electron has more penetration power than <math><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi></math> electron<br />(IV) atomic radius of <math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi></math> is more than <math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></math><br />(atomic number <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mo>:</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></math> )<br />The correct statements are."
  },
